Skip -fcomplete-member-pointers for mingw clang builds.

That flag is added by chromium for clang on Windows to enforce MSVC-ABI
safety (https://crbug.com/847724). It is incompatible with libstdc++'s
std::function implementation, which declares the member-pointer base
class std::_Undefined_class as a forward declaration only. With
libstdc++ 16.1 + clang the result is:

  error: member pointer has incomplete base type 'std::_Undefined_class'

GCC ignores the unknown flag, which is why the GCC build is unaffected.
For mingw clang + libstdc++ (Itanium ABI on the C++ side) the flag has
no useful effect and must be disabled.

--- a/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
+++ b/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
@@ -1006,7 +1006,7 @@ config("compiler") {
   # This flag enforces that member pointer base types are complete. It helps
   # prevent us from running into problems in the Microsoft C++ ABI (see
   # https://crbug.com/847724).
-  if (is_clang && target_os != "chromeos" && (is_win || use_custom_libcxx)) {
+  if (is_clang && target_os != "chromeos" && (is_msvc || use_custom_libcxx)) {
     cflags += [ "-fcomplete-member-pointers" ]
   }
 
